  I recommend [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m with the in-memory module in front of it: consumers only ever talk to the module, so
  storage is an implementation detail and moving to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m later is a one-line change rather than a hunt through
  every form. The persistence choice only leaks if you let forms touch [38;5;153mStorage[39m directly, which the design won't.

  One caution worth stating now: a user id in browser storage is an identifier, not a credential. It tells you who the
  client [3mclaims[0m to be and nothing more, so nothing security-relevant should trust it — the server still has to authorize
  every request on its own. That's fine for tracking; I just don't want the stored id mistaken for proof of login
  later.

