[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: the original motivation was "so we can track who logged in," and I want to be sure I build the right thing.
  There's a difference between [3mknowing[0m the current user and [3mrecording[0m login events, and they're separate pieces of work.

  - [1mJust knowing[0m — the session module exposes the current user, and the existing [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m in [38;5;153mlogin[39m keeps being the
    only record. Nothing new to build beyond the session layer.
  - [1mRecording events too[0m — a small tracking seam that emits a "user logged in" event (id, timestamp) somewhere durable
    or analytics-bound. That's a second subsystem with its own destination, failure behavior, and privacy questions.

  I recommend scoping this spec to [3mknowing[0m — the session layer is the prerequisite for any tracking, later forms need it
  regardless, and an event sink is straightforward to add on top once there's a current user to attribute events to.
  Building both at once doubles the surface for something you may want to route to an analytics vendor rather than
  hand-roll.
